WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.740
Hey, welcome to Fascinating Frontiers Episode 141.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.720
Today is July 24th, 2026.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.900
The universe has been busy, let's get onto it.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.660
Hubble has found the first missing black hole

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.440
inside a famous star cluster

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:18.180
by combining two decades of data with new infrared views.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.940
20 years of Hubble archival images

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:24.360
combined with James Webb Space Telescope infrared data

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.720
revealed a black hole in a well-studied

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:27.720
globular cluster.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.260
The detection relied on measuring stellar velocities

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:34.860
that could only be explained by an unseen massive object.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.500
This marks the first confirmed stellar mass black hole

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.300
in such an environment where dynamical ejection

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.300
was long predicted.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.060
Follow-up spectroscopy will refine the mass estimate

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:47.680
and test formation models.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.540
Hubble's detection relied on two decades

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:54.260
of proper motion measurements plus fresh infrared imaging.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.540
The unseen object's gravitational influence

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.400
on surrounding stars produced velocity signatures

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.340
that no other explanation fits.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.460
This single find supports models predicting

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.180
that many clusters once held more black holes

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:11.260
than they do today, most having been ejected

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:12.780
through dynamical interactions.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.860
The next step is targeted spectroscopy

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:17.700
to pin down the black hole's mass

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.160
and test whether similar objects lurk

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.260
in other clusters already imaged by Hubble and Webb.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.020
If every star in a globular cluster

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:29.260
moved exactly as expected from visible mass alone,

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.980
the cluster would fly apart or collapse.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.780
Instead, precise tracking shows a handful of stars

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.820
whipping around at speeds that require

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:41.020
an invisible central mass of several solar masses.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.840
Picture riding alongside one of those stars for a century.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.820
Its path bends sharply every few years

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.100
as it swings past an unseen companion.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.260
While neighboring stars trace gentler arcs

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:55.580
around the cluster's overall center of mass,

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.540
Hubble's repeated imaging over 20 years

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.640
measures those tiny shifts in position

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.680
down to a few milli arc seconds,

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.100
turning the cluster into a vast natural laboratory

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.020
where orbital mechanics acts like a balanced scale.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.120
The method works because of black holes gravity

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.140
dominates within a few light days

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:18.120
even though its event horizon is only tens of kilometers across.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.720
Yet the same data leave open a stubborn question.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.360
Why do some clusters appear to retain their black holes

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.240
while others seem to have ejected nearly all of them

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:31.080
through three body encounters billions of years ago?

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.400
Recent mapping of rift flanks on Venus

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.440
shows surfaces that formed within the last few hundred thousand years.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.240
The features appear alongside known volcanic sites

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.220
suggesting ongoing internal heat flow.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.560
Researchers used radar data from earlier orbiter passes

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.920
to measure flank slopes and compare them with older terrain.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.640
The work challenges the long held view

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.980
that Venus has been geologically quiet

00:02:57.980 --> 00:02:59.740
for hundreds of millions of years.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.980
Next steps include higher resolution imaging

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.880
from upcoming missions to pinpoint active vents.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:09.360
A cement-like material designed for lunar construction

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.180
was exposed outside the station for six months.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.520
Upon return the samples measured up to 35% stronger

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.660
than identical controls kept on Earth.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.980
The improvement is attributed to vacuum exposure

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:25.700
and thermal cycling that altered the material's microstructure.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.900
The experiment tests whether lunar regolith

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:31.240
can be centered into durable building blocks

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:33.040
without Earth-supplied water.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.040
Further tests will examine radiation effects

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.440
on the same formulation.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:40.460
Two satellites have completed commissioning

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:43.180
and joined the Galileo navigation constellation.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.060
Their addition improves coverage, redundancy,

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.840
and signal availability for European users.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.660
Four first-generation satellites remain to be launched

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:55.520
with the next pair scheduled for later this year.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.400
The expanded constellation strengthens Europe's independent

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.300
positioning, navigation, and timing capability.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.240
The Air Force Life Cycle Management Center

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:08.440
will now manage development and sustainment of receivers

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.260
for the encrypted military GPS signal.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.400
The move follows earlier program delays and cost growth.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.340
The receivers provide positioning and timing

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:21.020
for weapon systems and secure communications.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.900
Officials expect the center's oversight

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.780
to stabilize requirements and speed integration

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:28.080
across platforms.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.540
Delivery milestones for the next receiver generation

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:32.860
remain under review.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:36.700
Observations show a comet whose rotation has reversed,

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.740
likely from uneven outgassing torques.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:42.480
The changing spin could stress the nucleus

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.920
enough to cause fragmentation on future solar approaches.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:48.600
The object is one of many studied

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:52.200
to understand how comets evolve after repeated heating cycles.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.880
Continued monitoring will track whether the spin state

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.120
stabilizes or accelerates breakup.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:05.040
The Curiosity blog for souls 4,954 to 4,960

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.000
highlights collaboration between science planners

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.100
and rover engineers at the University of New Brunswick.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:15.520
The AP-XS instrument continues to measure rock chemistry

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.620
while engineers manage power and mobility trade-offs.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:22.080
Past and present engineering contributions are credited

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:23.800
with keeping the rover productive

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:25.760
after more than a decade on Mars.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.200
The post notes upcoming drive and drill plans

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.540
that depend on this ongoing coordination.

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:35.460
NASA reassigned the Sunrise Helio Physics mission

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.720
from a Vulcan Centaur to a SpaceX Falcon Heavy.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:41.660
The change follows earlier vehicle delays

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:44.900
and allows the six satellite radio interferometer

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:46.800
to reach its planned orbit sooner.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:49.820
The mission will study solar radio bursts

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:53.360
from a vantage 1.5 million kilometers from Earth.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:57.260
Launch is now expected on the new vehicle in the coming year.

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:02.640
An image of NGC 7,293, the Helix Nebula,

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:07.480
was assembled from 24.3 hours of H alpha, O3,

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:11.480
and LRGB data taken at farm tivoli, Namibia.

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:16.240
The planetary nebula lies roughly 650 light years away

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:18.260
and surrounds a hot white dwarf.

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:21.620
The long integration reveals faint outer shells

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:23.780
and intricate filamentary structure.

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:27.640
The result demonstrates what dedicated amateur equipment

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.940
can achieve under dark southern skies.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.600
The Copernicus Sentinel-2 satellite

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:36.220
imaged the Goldfields Esperance region in Western Australia.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:39.460
The false color view highlights rectangular crop

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:42.100
and pasture fields set against red soils

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:43.600
and scattered salt lakes.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:46.880
Data from the mission's multispectral instrument

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:48.960
allow tracking of vegetation health

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.280
and irrigation patterns across thousands of square kilometers.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:55.740
The scene demonstrates how routine Sentinel passes

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:58.840
support agricultural monitoring in arid zones.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:02.740
A NASA space telescope used a novel detection method

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:06.200
to identify a planet that had evaded earlier surveys.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:09.280
The technique compensates for stellar activity

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:11.980
that masked the planet's gravitational signature.

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:15.480
Confirmation came after multiple observing seasons

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.020
ruled out false positives.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:21.140
The discovery adds to the census of worlds orbiting stars

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:23.080
already known to host planets.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:27.360
Astronomers note that searches for extraterrestrial intelligence

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:30.320
have largely avoided a portion of the radio spectrum

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:32.280
that could carry artificial signals.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:35.240
New receiver technology now allows

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.220
efficient coverage of those frequencies.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:40.080
Pilot observations are planned to test

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.320
whether previously inaccessible channels

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:43.940
contain narrow-band emissions.

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:47.040
The approach could expand the parameter space

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:48.760
for techno-signature hunts.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:52.180
The Department of Energy has allocated high-asset

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:54.200
low-enriched uranium for upcoming

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:56.080
space nuclear power systems.

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:59.840
The fuel will support propulsion and surface power concepts

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:02.060
that require higher operating temperatures

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:03.500
than traditional reactors.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:07.360
Delivery timelines align with plan demonstration flights

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:08.540
later this decade.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:12.080
The move reduces reliance on foreign fuel sources

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:14.240
for US space nuclear projects.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:17.300
A new government accountability office

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:20.180
report notes progress on several NASA programs

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:23.420
while warning of continued cost growth and launch delays.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.180
The assessment covers major projects

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:28.880
including human space flight and science missions.

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:31.800
Agency officials are reviewing the findings

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:34.600
to adjust baselines and contractor oversight.

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:37.060
The report will inform upcoming

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:39.040
congressional budget deliberations.

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:41.720
Data from the Maven Orbiter show that

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:45.080
certain Martian auroras formed through magnetic reconnection,

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:48.400
a process scaled down from Earth's magnetosphere.

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:51.040
The observations captured electrons

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:53.600
accelerated along crustal magnetic fields

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:54.820
in the southern hemisphere.

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:57.920
The findings explain why auroras appear

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.320
even without a global magnetic field.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.680
Continued Maven measurements will map how often

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:06.820
reconnection occurs under varying solar wind conditions.

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:10.340
New data keep reshaping how we read the sky.

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:12.740
One precise measurement at a time.

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:15.900
Keep an eye on the next round of Maven data releases

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:17.880
for more details on how often

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:20.020
those miniature reconnection events occur.

